### Missing required array in inputSchema
### Throwing from Tool handlers instead of isError
### Returning full DB tables without pagination
### Prompt Templates that define agent identity
### Forwarding server modelPreferences in sampling
### No Roots handler for filesystem servers
### Connecting community servers without reading manifest
### One MCP client per node instead of per server
`;
---

<LessonLayout
  title="L26 — MCP Server Interoperability"
>
  <LessonHeader
    num="L26"
    title="MCP Server Interoperability"
    subtitle="MCP is a two-way contract. Servers declare what they expose — Tools, Resources, Prompt Templates. Clients declare what they provide back — Roots, Sampling. Both sides negotiate capabilities at connect time."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <!-- ── Introduction ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What MCP standardises</h2>
    <p>
      Think about what you had to do before MCP existed. Every agent contained its own
      hardcoded integrations — functions you wrote, wired up, and deployed alongside the
      agent. When a second agent needed the same database query, you copied the code. When
      a teammate's Python script wanted to reuse it, they couldn't. When Claude Desktop
      wanted to surface it to end users, there was no standard way to plug in.
    </p>
    <p>
      MCP (the Model Context Protocol) solves this the same way HTTP solved it for web
      services: by agreeing on a common language that every participant speaks. Once a
      capability is wrapped in an MCP server, any MCP client in the world — your Node.js
      agents, Python scripts, Claude Desktop, tools that don't even exist yet — can
      discover and use it without any custom adapter code.
    </p>
    <p>MCP standardises exactly three things:</p>

    <div class="mcp-grid">
      <div class="mcp-card">
        <div class="mcp-label">Discovery</div>
        <p><code>listTools()</code>, <code>listResources()</code>, <code>listPrompts()</code> — any client asks the server what it offers at runtime. No hardcoded lists in the agent.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mcp-card">
        <div class="mcp-label">Description</div>
        <p>Each primitive carries a JSON Schema for inputs and a human-readable description the LLM reads to decide when and how to use it. Same format for every server.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mcp-card">
        <div class="mcp-label">Connection</div>
        <p>Two transports: <strong>stdio</strong> (local process) and <strong>HTTP+SSE</strong> (deployed service). Protocol is identical — swap transports without changing handler code.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  subgraph CLI ["MCP Client — your agent"]
    AG["Agent code"]:::client
    subgraph CPROV ["Client provides TO server"]
      RO["🌲 Roots\\npath access scope"]:::cap
      SA["🧠 Sampling\\nLLM on demand"]:::cap
    end
  end

  subgraph SRV ["MCP Server"]
    subgraph SPROV ["Server exposes TO client"]
      T["🔧 Tools\\ncallable fns"]:::prim
      R["📄 Resources\\nreadable data"]:::prim
      P["💬 Prompts\\nparam messages"]:::prim
    end
  end

  SRV -->|"listTools / listResources / listPrompts\\ncallTool / readResource / getPrompt"| CLI
  CLI -->|"listRoots\\nsampling/createMessage"| SRV

  classDef client fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef prim   fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
