    <!-- ── Tool calling vs MCP ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Tool calling vs MCP — what's the difference?</h2>
    <p>
      Both approaches result in the same thing at runtime: the LLM receives a
      <code>tool_use</code> block, your code executes the function, and the result comes
      back as a message. The LLM doesn't know or care which approach you used.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difference is <strong>where the tool lives</strong> — and what that means for
      who else can use it. With direct tool calling, the tool is defined inside the agent's
      codebase. It's fast and simple, but it belongs to that agent alone. The moment a
      second agent needs the same capability, you're either duplicating code or creating
      a tightly-coupled shared dependency. MCP moves the tool to a standalone server that
      any MCP-compatible client can connect to — your agents, Claude Desktop, a Python
      colleague's script, anything.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Direct tool calling</th><th>MCP</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Tool lives</td><td>Inside the agent codebase</td><td>In a separate MCP server process</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Discovery</td><td>Hardcoded at build time</td><td>Dynamic: <code>listTools()</code> at runtime</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sharing</td><td>One agent only (copy to reuse)</td><td>Any MCP client — agents, Claude Desktop, CI</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Language</td><td>Same runtime as the agent</td><td>Any language — Python, Go, Rust</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Deploy</td><td>Change tool → redeploy agent</td><td>Change tool → redeploy server only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Use when</td><td>One agent, simple deployment</td><td>Shared tools, multi-agent, external access</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="direct-vs-mcp.ts" code={directVsMcpCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Start inline, extract when the second agent arrives">
      <p>
        Don't build an MCP server speculatively. Start with direct tool calling. Extract
        to MCP the moment a second agent needs the same tool — that's when the protocol
        overhead pays for itself.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="lesson-part">Part 1 — Building an MCP Server</div>
    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- ── Three primitives overview ─────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The three server primitives</h2>
    <p>
      An MCP server doesn't just expose tools. It can expose up to three fundamentally
      different types of things — and which type you use for a given piece of functionality
      is the most important design decision when building a server. Use the wrong one and
      you'll pay unnecessary token costs, return stale results, or introduce subtle prompt
      conflicts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each server announces which types it offers in its <code>capabilities</code> object
      at startup. A server can offer any combination of the three.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Primitive</th><th>Mental model</th><th>Client calls</th><th>When to use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tools</strong></td>
            <td>Verbs — the agent triggers them, something happens</td>
            <td><code>listTools()</code> · <code>callTool()</code></td>
            <td>Queries, API calls, writes, any action with a side effect</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Resources</strong></td>
            <td>Nouns — the agent reads them for context</td>
            <td><code>listResources()</code> · <code>readResource(uri)</code></td>
            <td>Static or semi-static reference data — catalogs, config, docs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Prompt Templates</strong></td>
            <td>Reusable parameterised message arrays</td>
            <td><code>listPrompts()</code> · <code>getPrompt(name, args)</code></td>
            <td>Shared prompt structure across multiple agents</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TD
  subgraph SRV ["MCP Server — what it exposes to clients"]
    T["🔧 Tools\\ncallable · side effects · queries\\nparams vary per call"]:::prim
    R["📄 Resources\\nreadable data · URI-identified\\nstatic or schedule-refreshed"]:::prim
    P["💬 Prompt Templates\\nparameterised messages · server-side logic\\nshared across agents"]:::prim
  end

  classDef prim fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
`} />

    <!-- ── Primitive 1: Tools ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Primitive 1 — Tools (callable functions)</h2>
    <p>
      Tools are the primitive you'll use most often. The mental model is simple: a Tool
      is something that <em>does something</em>. The agent calls it, the server runs code,
      and a result comes back. Database queries, external API calls, file operations, writes,
      sending notifications — if an action needs to happen and something in the world might
      change as a result, it's a Tool.
    </p>
    <p>
      The implementation pattern that matters most is what happens when something goes
      wrong. LLMs make mistakes — they'll call a tool with a missing parameter, an
      out-of-range value, or an ID that doesn't exist. Your handler needs to deal with
      this without crashing the conversation. The approach: validate first with Zod's
      <code>safeParse()</code>, then if validation fails, <strong>return an error as
      content</strong> rather than throwing. When you return
      <code>&#123; isError: true, content: [...] &#125;</code>, the agent reads your
      message and decides what to do next — retry, ask the user, try a different approach.
      When you throw, the MCP client receives a protocol-level error and the agent loop dies.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="weather-tool-server.ts" code={mcpToolServerCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Return errors as content — never throw from a handler">
      <p>
        An unhandled exception in a Tool handler sends a protocol-level error to the
        client — the agent crashes rather than reasoning about the failure.
        Always <code>return &#123; isError: true, content: [&#123; type: 'text', text: '...' &#125;] &#125;</code>.
        The agent reads that message and can decide whether to retry, escalate, or inform the user.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Always declare the required array in inputSchema">
      <p>
        JSON Schema's <code>required</code> array is a separate field from
        <code>properties</code>. If you omit it, every parameter becomes optional —
        the LLM will happily call your tool without the fields it actually needs,
        producing confusing errors with no hint of what went wrong. Always list every
        non-optional field explicitly in <code>required</code>.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- ── Primitive 2: Resources ─────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Primitive 2 — Resources (readable data)</h2>
    <p>
      Resources answer a different question than Tools do. Where a Tool says "do this",
      a Resource says "here is data you can read". The agent fetches a resource by URI
      — <code>product://catalog</code>, <code>config://pricing-rules</code>,
      <code>customer://id/123</code> — and receives text, JSON, or binary content back.
      Think of it as giving the agent a file it can open.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>noun/verb test</strong> is the clearest way to tell them apart. If you're
      thinking of something as a <em>thing</em> — the catalog, the pricing rules, the
      shipping zones — it's probably a Resource. If you're thinking of it as an
      <em>action</em> — search the catalog for "red widget", look up the price for SKU-123
      — it's a Tool.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>timing test</strong> gives you a second check: does the answer depend on
      what the agent knows right now, or is it the same data no matter when you ask? The
      product catalog refreshed every night is the same content for every agent on every
      call — that's a Resource. The order status for order #ORD-42 that the customer just
      mentioned in the conversation — the ID came from the live dialogue — that's a Tool.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="resource-handler.ts (partial server)" code={mcpResourcesCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Design URIs to reflect your data domain — and paginate">
      <p>
        Keep URIs stable — clients may cache them. Use scheme prefixes that map to your
        data domain: <code>product://</code>, <code>config://</code>,
        <code>customer://id/123</code>. For large datasets, always paginate or filter
        at the database level before returning. A 50 000-row product table returned as
        one resource will overflow the context window and cost more than the entire
        conversation is worth.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- ── Primitive 3: Prompt Templates ─────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Primitive 3 — Prompt Templates (parameterised messages)</h2>
    <p>
      Prompt Templates are the most counterintuitive primitive because they feel like
      something that should live in the agent, not on a server. But think about what
      happens when your Chat Specialist and Email Specialist both send order confirmations
      to customers. They're different agents, possibly different codebases — but the
      customer experience should be consistent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without Prompt Templates you have two bad options: duplicate the prompt text in both
      agents and try to keep them in sync, or share it through a library that requires
      redeploying both agents whenever a product manager wants to adjust the wording. With a
      Prompt Template, the prompt logic lives on the MCP server. Each agent calls
      <code>getPrompt('order-confirmation', &#123; customer_name, order_id, ... &#125;)</code>
      and gets back a ready-to-send <code>messages</code> array. Update the template once
      on the server — both agents pick it up on the next call, no deployment required.
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice the <code>channel</code> argument in the code below: the same template produces
      short chat-friendly text or longer email-appropriate text based on a single parameter.
      One template, two behaviours, zero duplication across agents.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="prompt-handler.ts (partial server)" code={mcpPromptsCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Templates must describe what to write — not who the agent is">
      <p>
        A Prompt Template's <code>messages</code> array is injected into the conversation.
        If the template includes role instructions ("You are a banking agent") that conflict
        with the calling agent's system prompt, the LLM receives two contradictory identities.
        Keep templates task-focused — they describe <em>what to produce</em>, not
        <em>who the agent is</em>. Identity belongs in the system prompt, not in shared templates.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- ── Which primitive? ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Which primitive should I use?</h2>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The data or action is…</th><th>Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>An action that runs computation or has a side effect</td>
            <td><strong>Tool</strong></td>
            <td><code>check_inventory</code>, <code>create_order</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Reference data — same content regardless of agent state, refreshed on a schedule</td>
            <td><strong>Resource</strong></td>
            <td><code>product://catalog</code>, <code>config://pricing-rules</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A query whose parameters come from the live conversation</td>
            <td><strong>Tool</strong></td>
            <td><code>get_order_status(order_id)</code> — the ID came from what the user just said</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A prompt structure used by multiple agents, or one that non-engineers need to edit</td>
            <td><strong>Prompt Template</strong></td>
            <td><code>order-confirmation</code> shared by Chat and Email Specialists</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A prompt used by exactly one agent, never changing independently</td>
            <td><strong>Inline system prompt</strong></td>
            <td>No MCP needed — keep it in the agent directly</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Transport mechanisms ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Transport mechanisms</h2>
    <p>
      The MCP protocol defines <em>what</em> gets communicated between client and server.
      Transport defines <em>how</em> those bytes physically move. Your server handler code
      — all the <code>setRequestHandler</code> calls for Tools, Resources, and Prompts —
      is completely identical regardless of which transport you use. The only thing that
      changes is the two lines of setup at startup.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Transport</th><th>How it works</th><th>Use when</th><th>Avoid when</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>stdio</strong></td>
            <td>Agent spawns server as child process; messages flow over stdin/stdout</td>
            <td>Local tools, development, Claude Desktop, single-agent deployment</td>
            <td>Multiple agent instances — each spawns its own process, no sharing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>HTTP+SSE</strong></td>
            <td>Client opens SSE stream (GET), sends requests via POST; server pushes events</td>
            <td>Deployed service, multiple agent instances sharing one server, cross-machine access</td>
            <td>Local-only tools where the HTTP overhead is not worth it</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TD
  subgraph LOCAL ["stdio — local / development"]
    A1["Agent process"]:::client -->|"stdin / stdout"| S1["MCP server\\n(child process)"]:::server
  end
  subgraph PROD ["HTTP+SSE — deployed / multi-instance"]
    A2["Agent instance 1"]:::client
    A3["Agent instance 2"]:::client
    S2["MCP server\\n(HTTP :3100)"]:::server
    A2 -->|"GET /sse · POST /message"| S2
    A3 -->|"GET /sse · POST /message"| S2
  end

  classDef client fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef server fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
`} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">stdio — scenario: local development tool</h3>
    <p>
      The stdio transport is what you've been seeing throughout this lesson. The client
      spawns the server as a child process and they communicate over stdin/stdout. No
      network config, no port to allocate, no HTTP stack. The natural choice for local
      tools, development workflows, and Claude Desktop integrations.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">HTTP+SSE — scenario: shared microservice</h3>
    <p>
      HTTP+SSE becomes necessary the moment multiple agent processes need to share a single
      server. Imagine the Chat Specialist and Email Specialist both running as separate
      NestJS workers. With stdio, each process spawns its own copy of
      <code>sales-tools-server</code> — two processes with duplicate code and no shared
      state. With HTTP+SSE, you deploy one server instance and both agents connect to it
      over the network. The handler code below is identical to the stdio server — only
      the transport wiring at the bottom is different.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="sales-tools-http-server.ts" code={httpServerCode} />
    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="mcp-http-client.ts" code={httpClientCode} />

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <div class="lesson-part">Part 2 — Using an MCP Server (Client Side)</div>
    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- ── Connecting and discovering ────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Connecting and discovering</h2>
    <p>
      Connecting to an MCP server always follows the same sequence: connect, discover,
      then use. Your agent asks the server what it offers — tools, resources, prompt
      templates — and works with whatever it finds. The code below shows the full cycle.
      Read it once as a whole; the sections above cover each primitive in depth.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="mcp-client.ts" code={mcpClientCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="MCP schema → Anthropic schema is a direct pass-through">
      <p>
        MCP tool <code>inputSchema</code> is JSON Schema. The Anthropic SDK's
        <code>input_schema</code> is also JSON Schema. No transformation needed — cast
        and pass it straight through. The same applies to the <code>messages</code> array
        returned by <code>getPrompt()</code>: it is already in Anthropic message format.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- ── One client, filter per node ───────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">One MCP client per server — filter tools per node</h2>
    <p>
      Here's a question worth pausing on: if your LangGraph graph has five nodes and each
      one needs to call an MCP server, should you create five MCP connections — one per node?
    </p>
    <p>
      You shouldn't. An MCP connection involves either spawning a child process (stdio) or
      opening a network session (HTTP+SSE) — both are meaningful setup work. The right
      pattern is to <strong>create one client per server when the graph initialises</strong>,
      call <code>listTools()</code> once, cache the result, and reuse that same connection
      across every node for the lifetime of the graph.
    </p>
    <p>
      But here's the subtlety. Every tool description you pass to
      <code>messages.create(&#123; tools &#125;)</code> gets loaded into the LLM's context
      as tokens — name, description, and input schema together run 200–500 tokens per tool.
      If your MCP server exposes 12 tools but a given node only needs 2, passing all 12
      wastes roughly 3 000 tokens on every call. Multiply across thousands of invocations
      and the cost adds up fast. Worse: giving the LLM tools it can't usefully act on here
      leads to noisier, less reliable decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The solution: <strong>connect once, filter per node</strong>. Cache the full tool list
      at startup, then filter it down to exactly what each node needs before each LLM call.
      The "3–5 tools per node" rule still applies — it just operates at the
      <em>tools passed per call</em> level, not the number of connections.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What to do</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Graph init</strong></td>
            <td><code>listTools()</code> once → convert → cache as <code>allTools</code></td>
            <td>Connection setup is expensive; discovery tokens paid once, not per call</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Per LLM call</strong></td>
            <td>Filter <code>allTools</code> to the subset this node actually uses</td>
            <td>Each tool description ≈ 300 tokens in context; irrelevant tools waste cost and degrade decisions</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tool execution</strong></td>
            <td>All nodes share the same <code>mcpClient</code> instance</td>
            <td>Execution reuses the established connection — no reconnect overhead</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="mcp-per-node-filtering.ts" code={mcpPerNodeCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Tool descriptions cost tokens on every LLM call">
      <p>
        A filesystem MCP server exposes ~8 tools; a sales server exposes ~6.
        Passing all 14 to every node = 14 × ~300 tokens = ~4 200 token overhead
        per call. At 1 000 calls/day that's meaningful cost — plus degraded decision
        quality. Filter ruthlessly. Each node should see only what it can actually use.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- ── Client capabilities ───────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What the MCP client provides back to the server</h2>
    <p>
      Most MCP tutorials stop after explaining what servers expose to clients. But the
      diagram at the top of this lesson shows two arrows for a reason. MCP is a two-way
      contract — clients also declare capabilities that servers can call on.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <strong>server-to-client direction</strong> (top arrow) is what you've been
      learning: servers expose Tools, Resources, and Prompt Templates. The
      <strong>client-to-server direction</strong> (bottom arrow) is what most tutorials
      miss: the client advertises capabilities <em>back</em> to the server, and
      well-designed servers use them. Both sides declare at connection time:
    </p>

    <div class="mcp-grid">
      <div class="mcp-card">
        <div class="mcp-label">Server declares (what it exposes)</div>
        <p><code>new Server(info, &#123; capabilities: &#123;<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;tools: &#123;&#125;,<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;resources: &#123;&#125;,<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;prompts: &#123;&#125;<br/>&#125; &#125;)</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="mcp-card">
        <div class="mcp-label">Client declares (what it provides back)</div>
        <p><code>new Client(info, &#123; capabilities: &#123;<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;roots: &#123; listChanged: true &#125;,<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;sampling: &#123;&#125;<br/>&#125; &#125;)</code></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Roots ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Roots — controlling filesystem access</h3>
    <p>
      Roots are the client's answer to "what parts of the filesystem are you allowed to
      touch?" When a client declares the <code>roots</code> capability, any filesystem-aware
      MCP server should call <code>listRoots()</code> before accessing any file path and
      stay strictly within the returned URIs. Without Roots, a filesystem server can
      potentially read or write any file on the machine your agent runs on.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are four configurations you'll encounter in practice:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Single path</strong></td>
            <td>One project folder; server stays inside it. Most common case.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Multiple paths</strong></td>
            <td>Monorepo or multi-service workspace — several directories, each sandboxed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Dynamic roots</strong></td>
            <td>Accessible paths are not fixed at build time — read from config or chosen by the user at runtime. Use <code>listChanged: true</code> so the server re-fetches when roots update.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Empty roots</strong></td>
            <td>Explicitly block all filesystem access from this server. Register the handler but return an empty array.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="mcp-roots-configurations.ts" code={mcpRootsCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Always declare a Roots handler for filesystem servers">
      <p>
        If you register the <code>roots</code> capability but provide no handler, the
        server's behaviour when it calls <code>listRoots()</code> is undefined. If you
        skip the capability entirely, some servers fall back to accessing arbitrary paths
        without asking. Always register an explicit handler — even if it returns an empty
        array to block all access.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <!-- ── Sampling ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Sampling — server-initiated LLM calls</h3>
    <p>
      Sampling is the most powerful and the most dangerous client capability. In the normal
      MCP flow, the agent calls the server — the agent asks, the server answers. Sampling
      inverts this: the <em>server</em> can request an LLM call from the <em>client</em>.
      The server sends a <code>sampling/createMessage</code> request, the client's handler
      runs, and the LLM result goes back to the server.
    </p>
    <p>
      This opens a door. A malicious server can use sampling to inject arbitrary messages
      into your LLM context, trigger unlimited inference at your cost, or exfiltrate
      information from the agent's state. The client holds all the controls: you choose
      the model, you set the token cap, you validate the request content, and you decide
      whether to fulfil it at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      The four scenarios below cover the range from fully trusting to fully blocking. In
      practice you'll usually pick Scenario A for your own servers and Scenario D — or
      simply not registering sampling — for untrusted community servers.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="mcp-sampling-scenarios.ts" code={mcpSamplingCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="You choose the model — never let the server choose">
      <p>
        The <code>modelPreferences</code> field in a sampling request is advisory.
        Ignore it. Always specify your own model and token cap in the handler.
        If you blindly forward the server's model preference, an adversarial server
        can route your inference to a more expensive model and inflate your bill.
        Log all sampling invocations — anomaly detection catches abuse patterns early.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

