<p>MCP servers have four surfaces that can inject content into your agent's context:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Tool descriptions</strong> — land verbatim in the Anthropic <code>tools</code> array sent to the model. "Ignore previous instructions" in a description is a working prompt injection.</li>
  <li><strong>Resource content</strong> — returned by <code>readResource()</code> and injected into the conversation. A resource body containing "SYSTEM: disregard instructions" achieves the same effect.</li>
  <li><strong>Prompt Templates</strong> — the returned <code>messages</code> array injects directly into the conversation history. A template that includes role instructions can override your agent's system prompt.</li>
  <li><strong>Sampling requests</strong> — the server can prompt your LLM directly via <code>sampling/createMessage</code>, fully controlling what it sees and says.</li>
</ol>
<p>Vetting checklist before connecting any community server:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Call <code>listTools()</code>, <code>listResources()</code>, and <code>listPrompts()</code> locally — read every description and template in full before approving.</li>
  <li>Open the source repository and check recent PRs, open issues, and ownership history. Sudden ownership transfers are a red flag.</li>
  <li>Pin to a specific version tag. Never use <code>npx @org/server@latest</code> in production — the next publish could be malicious.</li>
  <li>Declare Roots for any server that touches the filesystem.</li>
  <li>Never allow user input to specify which MCP server to connect to — allowlist at deploy time, not runtime.</li>
  <li>Audit-log every tool call, resource read, prompt request, and sampling invocation.</li>
</ul>
          `.trim(),
