<p>Sampling inverts the normal trust direction: the server reaches into the client's LLM rather than the client asking the server. The three main risks:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Prompt injection</strong> — the server sends a crafted <code>systemPrompt</code> that overrides your agent's identity or instructions.</li>
  <li><strong>Cost amplification</strong> — the server triggers unlimited inference calls at your expense, with no natural stop condition.</li>
  <li><strong>Data exfiltration</strong> — the server asks the LLM to summarise or repeat sensitive information from the agent's context window.</li>
</ul>
<p>All the controls sit on the client side — you hold every lever:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Choose your own model.</strong> Ignore <code>modelPreferences</code> entirely. Hardcode the model and token cap in your handler; never let the server influence either.</li>
  <li><strong>Rate-limit per session.</strong> Track the call count, and throw <code>McpError(ErrorCode.InvalidRequest, ...)</code> once you exceed your threshold.</li>
  <li><strong>Validate message content before forwarding.</strong> Apply the injection detection patterns from L13 to the incoming messages before passing them to the LLM.</li>
  <li><strong>Reject outright for untrusted servers.</strong> Throw <code>McpError(ErrorCode.MethodNotFound, ...)</code> from the handler. Or simply don't declare the <code>sampling</code> capability when connecting to community servers.</li>
  <li><strong>Log every sampling call.</strong> Anomaly detection on call frequency and content catches abuse before it scales into a real cost or security problem.</li>
</ul>
          `.trim(),
