<p>Design each server connection to fail independently of the others.</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Connect in isolation.</strong> Wrap each server's connect call in its own try/catch. A failing connection must not prevent the remaining servers from initialising.</li>
  <li><strong>Classify mandatory vs optional at design time.</strong> <code>sales-tools-server</code> is required: if it's unavailable at startup, fail fast and alert on-call. A weather enrichment server is optional: log the error and continue without it — the agent can still process orders.</li>
  <li><strong>Build a capability registry.</strong> After all connections complete, record exactly which tools, resources, and prompts were successfully discovered. Before every primitive call, check the registry. If the capability isn't there, fall back gracefully or inform the user rather than letting the LLM call a tool that doesn't exist.</li>
  <li><strong>Plan for mid-session failures.</strong> For HTTP+SSE: implement a health-check loop and re-register primitives when the server comes back. For stdio: watch for the child process exit event and restart it automatically.</li>
  <li><strong>Tell the LLM what's unavailable.</strong> If <code>check_inventory</code> goes down mid-session, add a note to the system prompt: "inventory service currently unavailable." The LLM can then explain this to the user rather than hallucinating stock levels.</li>
</ol>
          `.trim(),
